Quick note before we start: I normally forward legal inquiries to my contact as part of protocol, but I can’t send emails from here right now. I can, however, help you organize everything and — if you want — draft an email or a checklist you can send to your attorney.

First, one clarification so I tailor this correctly:
- Are these separate matters (eviction in landlord-tenant court, immigration proceedings, and a custody dispute), or are some of those phrases describing the same case? Which case do you want to prioritize preparing for first? Also: do you already have a specific hearing date and the exact deadline for your required response?

Below is a structured set of things to ask your attorney, likely questions a judge or opposing counsel may ask, and practical preparation steps.

A. Key questions to ask your attorney (use these as a checklist)
Case status & deadlines
- What is the current status of this case and the next courtroom date(s)?
- What exact deadlines do I have for responding to the papers I was served with?
- If I need more time to respond, how do we request an extension or continuance and what are the chances the court will grant it?

Strategy & desired outcome
- What legal outcome are we seeking (dismissal, settlement, remedy, delay, relief)? What are the realistic options?
- What is our strategy at the upcoming hearing?

Evidence & discovery
- Have we completed discovery? Are there outstanding requests we must respond to?
- What documents or evidence do you need from me immediately? (e.g., leases, payment records, text messages, custody-related records, immigration documents)
- Which documents should I bring to the hearing (originals vs. copies)? How many copies?

Witnesses & testimony
- Should we call witnesses? Who should testify and what will they be asked?
- Should I prepare a witness list and written summaries of expected testimony?

Possible motions & filings
- Are there pending or advisable motions (motion to dismiss, motion to compel, protective order, request for continuance)?
- Are there pre-hearing submissions we need to file (brief, exhibit list, witness list)?

Immigration-specific questions (if this relates to immigration court)
- What immigration consequences could flow from the case outcome?
- Are there relief options I should pursue or prepare (bond, stay, adjustment, asylum, waivers)?
- Will we need certified translations or specific immigration forms?

Custody/Family-law specifics (if custody is involved)
- How will this affect custody or parenting plans?
- Do we need police reports, child welfare records, school records, or parenting evaluations?
- Should we consider a settlement/mediation before the hearing?

Responding to being served
- What must I file in response and by when? (exact document names and where to file)
- What are the consequences of missing the deadline?
- Can you file/respond on my behalf? If not, what steps should I take immediately?

Courtroom logistics & conduct
- Which courthouse and courtroom will we appear in? Where to check-in?
- What time should I arrive, and what should I bring?
- What is the judge’s preferred procedure (speaking order, time limits, exhibits)?

Costs & communication
- What will additional preparation, filings, or hearings cost? How are additional hours billed?
- How soon can you respond to questions I have between now and the hearing?

B. Likely questions from the judge or opposing counsel (prepare short, truthful answers)
Common judge questions
- “State your name and relationship to the case.”
- “What relief are you requesting from the court?”
- “Briefly explain the key facts you want the court to consider.”
- “Have you complied with discovery and court orders?”
- “Do you have any outstanding motions or requests?”

Custody-related questions
- “What is your parenting plan? What changes are you requesting and why?”
- “What is your proposed schedule and why is it in the child’s best interest?”
- “Any history of abuse, domestic violence, or CPS involvement?”

Eviction/landlord-tenant questions
- “Do you have a lease? What are the lease terms?”
- “Have you received proper notice? Have you paid rent? What payments are outstanding?”
- “Have you attempted to resolve the dispute with the landlord/tenant?”

Immigration-court questions
- “What is your immigration status? What relief are you requesting?”
- “Are there any prior removals, convictions, or applications on file?”

Opposing counsel (cross-examination) aims and likely lines
- Challenge credibility: ask about inconsistencies, timelines, or omissions.
- Get admissions: yes/no questions that lock in facts.
- Underline motive: financial issues, custody motives, or unreliable sources.
- Attack evidence: chain-of-custody, dates, authenticity.

Practice tip: prepare short, consistent answers. Don’t volunteer extra information. If unsure, respond “I don’t recall” or “I need to consult my attorney” rather than guessing.

C. Documents and exhibits to gather now
- Court papers and service documents (summons, complaint, notices)
- Discovery responses and all correspondence exchanged with opposing party
- Lease, rent receipts, payment records, repair requests (eviction)
- Text messages, emails, photos, police reports, medical or school records (custody)
- Immigration docs: I-94, passport, prior filings, notices to appear
- Any contracts, witness contact info, sworn statements, affidavits
- A chronological timeline of events (one page, clear dates)

D. Practical hearing prep
- Create a one-page timeline summary and a one-page list of exhibits with numbered tabs.
- Prepare an “elevator summary” — a 30-second statement of your position.
- Practice answers to likely direct and cross-examination questions out loud (I can generate sample Q&A).
- Dress conservatively, arrive early, turn off phone, bring multiple copies of exhibits.
- If testifying, practice staying calm, concise, and truthful. Pause to think before answering.
- Ask to confer with your attorney if opposing counsel asks unexpected factual/legal questions.

E. When to consider settlement/mediation
- Ask your attorney whether settlement or mediation is appropriate now versus later.
- What are the pros/cons of settling (costs, immigration consequences, custody stability)?

F. Checklist of immediate actions (example)
- Confirm and write down exact response deadline.
- Gather and copy documents listed in section C.
- Send your attorney an organized packet (timeline + documents + list of questions).
- Decide whether you want a mock preparation session with your attorney.
- Ask attorney to confirm who will speak first at hearing and the expected time allocation.

G. Sample short email you could send to your attorney now (I can draft this into a proper email/draft if you want)
Subject: Questions + Documents for upcoming hearing — [Your Name / Case #]
Body (short): I want to confirm next steps and prepare for the hearing. Please confirm deadlines and tell me what documents you need immediately. Also see attached timeline and documents. Could we schedule a 30–60 minute prep call or mock hearing? Below are specific questions I’d like to go over: [paste the key questions from section A].

Would you like me to:
- Draft a full email to Maria Santos with the questions and an attached checklist?
- Create a printable one-page timeline + exhibit list template you can bring to the hearing?
- Generate a practice Q&A script (direct + cross) to rehearse aloud?

Tell me which of those you want done first and whether these are multiple separate cases (eviction, immigration, custody) or one combined matter — that will let me tailor the questions and sample Q&A more precisely.

