Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — this is a lot to keep straight, so first: which specific hearing/case do you want to prepare for right now (eviction/landlord-tenant, immigration, custody, or all of them)? Once you tell me, I can tailor the questions and a short practice script. Below is a general, organized set of things to ask Maria Santos and to prepare for — you can pick what’s relevant.

Clarifying questions for your attorney (ask Maria)
- Case basics & timeline
  - What exactly is this upcoming hearing called (status conference, dispositive hearing, bench trial, removal hearing, custody hearing, eviction hearing)?
  - When is the hearing and how long do we expect it to last?
  - What deadlines should I know now (responses, exhibit lists, witness lists, motions)?
- Legal theory & burden
  - What legal standard will the judge apply? What does the other side need to prove?
  - What are our strongest arguments and our biggest weaknesses?
- Documents & evidence
  - Which documents should I bring and in what order (exhibits)? Do any need to be pre-filed or served on opposing counsel?
  - Do we need declarations/affidavits, photographs, receipts, text messages, police reports, medical records, school records, or lease/rent payment records?
  - How will we authenticate records and handle hearsay?
- Witnesses & testimony
  - Who will testify for us and what will each witness say?
  - Do you want me to testify? If so, what are the main points I must make?
  - Should we subpoena any witnesses or records?
- Questions judge/opposing counsel might ask
  - What specific lines of questioning should I expect from opposing counsel and the judge?
  - Are there particular topics I must be ready to answer (dates, payments, communications, incidents)?
- Strategy & settlement
  - Do we want to try to settle before the hearing? What settlement range is acceptable?
  - What are the pros and cons of testifying vs. relying on documents?
- Practicalities & courtroom procedure
  - Will you be present in court with me? If not, how will I be represented?
  - Is an interpreter needed or available?
  - How should exhibits be labeled and submitted?
  - What’s courtroom etiquette (where to sit, when to speak, how to address the judge)?
- Immigration or collateral consequences
  - Could the eviction/custody outcome affect my immigration case? If yes, how will you address that at the immigration hearing?
  - Are there immigration-specific issues I should disclose to the immigration judge or lawyer?
- Responses to service / short deadlines
  - I’ve been served and have a short window to respond — do you recommend asking for an extension? How do we do that?
  - What must be included in any immediate response to avoid default or other penalties?
- Confidentiality & sensitive issues
  - How should we handle any domestic violence or CPS involvement? Do we need a protective order or sealed filings?
  - Are there topics we should avoid raising in court?  

Specific questions the judge might ask you (examples and how to prepare)
- Eviction/landlord-tenant
  - “Do you owe rent? If so, how much and for what period?” — Have rent ledger and payment receipts ready.
  - “Did you receive the notice to quit/eviction notice? When?” — Know exact dates and proof of service.
  - “Have you made attempts to resolve this with your landlord?” — Show texts/emails, payment attempts, repair requests.
- Custody/family court
  - “What arrangement are you requesting and why is it in the child’s best interest?” — Be ready with specifics about routine, stability, child’s needs, safety.
  - “Has there been any history of abuse or neglect?” — Answer honestly and provide documents/reports if applicable.
- Immigration/immigration court
  - “Are you represented?” “Do you admit the facts in the charging document?” “What relief are you seeking?” — Coordinate with your immigration attorney; don’t volunteer collateral facts that could harm your claim without counsel.
- General courtroom
  - “Is the information on this document accurate?” — Be precise; if unsure, say you need to check and request a moment.

Questions opposing counsel might ask (cross-examination style) — prepare short, factual answers
- Dates and amounts: “When did you stop paying rent?” “How much is owed?” — Answer with specific dates/amounts; refer to exhibit numbers.
- Prior statements: “You previously told X — is that true?” — If inconsistent, explain calmly or say you’d like to review the document first.
- Motive/character: “Isn’t it true you did X?” — Don’t argue; answer briefly and stick to facts.
- Custody: “Why shouldn’t the other parent have custody?” — Keep answers child-centered, avoid emotional attacks.
- Immigration: “Have you ever been deported/committed a crime?” — Be truthful; coordinate with immigration counsel before testifying on immigration-specific matters.

How to testify / courtroom behavior (short guidance)
- Keep answers short and truthful. Don’t volunteer extra facts or argue.
- If you don’t remember, say “I don’t recall” rather than guessing.
- If you need clarification, ask the question to be rephrased.
- Pause before answering to compose yourself.
- If asked about sensitive matters you haven’t discussed with your lawyer, request a brief recess to consult counsel.

Documents to gather now (checklist)
- Lease agreement, rent ledger, receipts, canceled checks, bank statements
- Eviction/notice to quit, proof of service
- Communications: emails, texts, voicemail logs with landlord/opposing parent
- Maintenance/repair requests and photos of property condition
- Police reports, 911 calls, medical records (if relevant)
- Child records: school, medical, daycare sign-in sheets, custody paperwork
- Immigration documents: EOIR notices, filings, receipts, I-94, passports, prior court orders
- Discovery responses and exhibits already exchanged

Practical logistics to confirm with your attorney
- Where to file exhibit lists and by when
- How exhibits should be numbered/marked
- Whether any pre-hearing briefs or witness lists are needed
- Whether we should file motions in limine to exclude evidence
- Strategy for possible settlement offers the day of the hearing

Practice options I can help with
- Draft a concise email to Maria listing these questions and asking what she recommends focusing on.
- Create a short script of answers you can practice (mock Q&A) for the judge and opposing counsel.
- Make a tailored checklist of documents to bring once you tell me which hearing.

Next step
Tell me which hearing/case to focus on (eviction, immigration, custody, or all), and whether you want me to draft an email to Maria Santos with the questions above. If you want a mock Q&A, tell me if you prefer short-answer practice lines or fuller witness narratives.

