I can help — first, a quick clarification so I give the most useful, concrete prep: which specific proceeding do you want to prepare for right now?

- The landlord–tenant eviction hearing (Denver County), or
- The immigration court proceeding, or
- The custody matter (other parent), or
- Are these all connected and you need prep that covers overlap between them?

Tell me which one to focus on. If you want all three, I can give separate checklists.

Below are (1) focused questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos), (2) likely questions a judge or opposing counsel may ask in a landlord–tenant eviction, (3) an evidence checklist and immediate steps given a short response window, and (4) preparation tips (including mock questioning). I’ll also offer to draft an email to Maria with these questions if you’d like.

1) Key questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos)
- Case status & deadlines
  - What is our current case posture (answer deadline, motions outstanding, hearing date expected)?
  - Exactly when is my response due, and can we get an extension or continuance if needed?
  - What are the realistic timelines to the next hearing or eviction enforcement?

- Legal theory & strategy
  - What is our legal theory/defense against the eviction (nonpayment, improper notice, habitability, retaliation, procedural defects, etc.)?
  - What are the strengths and weaknesses of the case from our perspective?
  - What outcomes should I expect (dismissal, delay, settlement, conditional stay, money judgment)?

- Evidence & witnesses
  - What documents or evidence are essential (lease, notices, rent receipts, repair requests, photos, communications)?
  - Which witnesses should we call or get statements from?
  - How should we authenticate payments/receipts? Should we subpoena any documents?

- Procedural/technical
  - Are there any motions you recommend now (motion to dismiss, motion to strike, motion for continuance, stay pending appeal, emergency relief)?
  - If I decide to contest, should I file a written response or appear and argue only?
  - What are the costs/fees and retainer implications if this goes to hearing?

- Testimony & cross-examination
  - Do you recommend I testify? What are the pros/cons?
  - What questions is the judge likely to ask me at the hearing?
  - What questions will opposing counsel likely ask on cross-examination?
  - How should I answer difficult questions (e.g., rental arrears, notices received, prior statements)?

- Immigration & custody overlap (if relevant)
  - Does the eviction outcome affect my immigration case or custody matter? If so, how?
  - Should I disclose immigration status or custody-related facts in this hearing?
  - Are there strategic considerations to prevent adverse immigration consequences?

- Settlement/alternatives
  - Is settlement or a stipulated agreement advisable? What terms would be reasonable?
  - If we settle, how will that affect other proceedings (immigration/custody)?

- Logistics & communication
  - How will you update me? How quickly can I reach you with new info?
  - Can we schedule a mock Q&A before the hearing?

2) Likely questions the judge or opposing counsel may ask in a landlord–tenant eviction (useful to prepare short factual answers)
- About the lease/tenancy
  - Do you have a written lease? When did the lease start and end?
  - Who signed the lease? Who lives in the unit?

- About rent/payment
  - Have you paid rent? On what dates? How much is owed now?
  - Do you have proof of payments (bank statements, receipts, money orders)?
  - Did you give any rent in cash? Can you produce a receipt?

- About notices & communications
  - Did the landlord serve you with a written notice? When and how were you served?
  - Did you respond to any notices? Did you request repairs or engage the landlord?

- About condition/habitability & repairs
  - Were there maintenance or habitability problems? Did you notify the landlord?
  - Did the landlord make repairs? Did you withhold rent or repair and deduct (if applicable)?

- About move-out & possession
  - Have you moved out or are you still occupying the unit?
  - If you moved, when and why? If not, when do you plan to move?

- About credibility/contradictions
  - Did you tell anyone else different facts earlier? (Be ready to explain differences calmly.)
  - Do you have witnesses who can confirm your version?

- About damages & offsets
  - Are you claiming offsets for repairs, deposit disputes, or other damages?
  - How did you calculate the amount you claim or the amount they claim?

If the hearing intersects with custody or immigration, expect targeted questions relevant to those areas (e.g., who has custody of children, living arrangements, public benefits, ties to community) — discuss with Maria whether to answer or object.

3) Evidence checklist — gather these now
- Lease or rental agreement (all pages)
- Notices (pay or quit, cure/quit, termination notices) with dates and proof of service
- Rent payment proof: bank records, canceled checks, money order receipts, screenshots of payments (include dates, amounts)
- Communications: texts, emails, letters between you and landlord or property manager (include dates)
- Repair requests and maintenance logs (written requests, photos of issues, dates when landlord was notified)
- Photographs and videos of the unit (dates/time-stamped if possible)
- Utility bills, occupancy records, or other proof of residency
- Witness contact info and short written statements (neighbors, roommates, maintenance people)
- Copies of court filings, pleadings, discovery responses and any evidence produced by the other side
- Any prior agreements or payment plans
- If claiming offsets: receipts for repairs you paid for, invoices, etc.

4) Immediate steps to take given short window to respond
