Thanks — I can help you prepare. First a quick clarification so I tailor this correctly:

- Do you have multiple separate matters (an eviction case, a custody case, and an immigration matter), or are these issues all tied together in one proceeding?  
- Which deadline is imminent (service/response deadline or a hearing date)? How many days do you have to respond?

While you answer, here’s a focused, practical checklist and a ready-made set of questions to take to Maria Santos plus likely questions a judge or opposing counsel may ask you so you can practice short, honest answers.

What to bring to your attorney meeting (and to court)
- Case basics: copies of the complaint/summons, all discovery responses and documents exchanged, proof of service, and any orders already entered.  
- Documents/evidence: lease, rent receipts, cancelled checks, bank statements, repair requests, emails/texts/messages with landlord/other parent, police reports, photos/videos, medical/ school records (for custody), immigration forms/ notices, ID and immigration documents.  
- Witness list & contact info: anyone who can corroborate facts (neighbors, roommates, teachers, doctors).  
- Timeline: a one-page written chronology of key dates (move-in, payments, notices, incidents, service dates, discovery deadlines).  
- Notes on credibility issues: anything that could be used against you and your explanations (past arrests, missed payments, etc.).  
- Relief you want: what outcome you want (dismissal, stay, payment plan, custody arrangement, continuance, bond, etc.).

Questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos)
1. Case/Procedure & Timeline
   - What exact deadlines do I have now (response, pre-hearing filings, motions)?  
   - What steps will you take between now and the hearing? What do you need me to do and by when?  
   - Are there any quick procedural motions we should file (motion to dismiss, motion to continue, motion to compel, protective order)?  
2. Strategy & Risks
   - What is the overall defense/strategy you recommend and why? What are the weak points in our position?  
   - What are the likely remedies or orders the judge can enter in my case? Best, worst, and likely outcomes?  
   - If settlement is an option, what is an acceptable range for you to negotiate?  
3. Evidence & Witnesses
   - Which documents are most important for the hearing and how should they be organized/exhibited?  
   - Which witnesses should we call and what will they be asked to testify to?  
   - How should I preserve or obtain additional evidence (records, receipts, police reports)?  
4. Questions the Judge or Opposing Counsel Might Ask Me
   - What specific questions do you expect the judge to ask me? What answers should I prepare?  
   - What cross-examination lines could opposing counsel pursue? How should I answer sensitive questions (immigration status, custody history, missed rent, criminal history)?  
5. Immigration-Specific (if applicable)
   - Could the eviction or custody issues affect my immigration case? If yes, how and what protective steps do we take?  
   - Do you coordinate with an immigration attorney (if Maria is not your immigration lawyer)?  
6. Custody-Specific (if applicable)
   - What custody-related questions will the judge focus on (parental fitness, visitation, safety)?  
   - What documents or evidence will be decisive (school records, CPS reports, medical records)?  
7. Courtroom Preparation
   - Can we do a prep session/ mock examination with anticipated questions?  
   - What courtroom etiquette should I follow (how to address the judge, when to speak, how to handle objections)?  
8. Contingencies & Communication
   - If I get new evidence or someone contacts me, how should I handle it? Who do I call first?  
   - How will you update me and how quickly can I reach you before the hearing?

Likely questions the judge or opposing counsel may ask you (practice short, truthful answers)
- Eviction/landlord-related:
  - “When did you move in and under what terms (lease or month-to-month)?”  
  - “Have you received any written notices? When and how were they delivered?”  
  - “Did you pay rent for [month X]? Do you have receipts or proof?”  
  - “Were there repair requests? What did you do to notify the landlord?”  
  - “Why do you believe the eviction is improper?”  
- Custody-related (if appearing in same or related proceedings):
  - “What is your current parenting time and how does it affect the child?”  
  - “Are there concerns about safety, parenting ability, substance use, or supervision?”  
  - “What outcome are you asking the court to order for the child?”  
- Immigration-related (if the court will ask or it’s relevant):
  - “What is your immigration status?” (Answer succinctly; consult attorney on whether to answer substance.)  
  - “Are you currently in removal proceedings?”  
  - “Has anything in these proceedings affected your immigration case?”  
- General/cross-examination:
  - “Is this document authentic?” (If you can’t authenticate, say so and ask for time to verify.)  
  - “Have you told anyone else something different?” (Be consistent; don’t volunteer extra facts.)  
  - “Why should the court believe you?” (Give concise, factual reasons and point to evidence.)

Practical courtroom and prep tips
- Be concise and truthful. Short answers are better than long explanations.  
- Practice answers out loud with your attorney; avoid guessing. If you don’t know, say “I don’t recall” rather than guessing.  
- Don’t argue with the judge or interrupt opposing counsel; let your attorney object and speak.  
- Bring organized exhibit packets (court copy + judge copy + one for opposing counsel) with an exhibit list.  
- Ask your attorney for a quick script for introductions: how to state your name, relationship to case, and what you’re asking for.  
- Record dates/times of all new contacts and preserve messages/emails.

Immediate next actions I recommend
- Confirm and preserve all physical/electronic evidence now (screenshots with timestamps, back up emails/texts).  
- Make a short timeline document of facts and send it to Maria with copies of key documents.  
- Ask Maria for a preparation or mock Q&A session before the hearing.  
- If you want, I can draft a concise email you can send to Maria listing the questions above and attaching your timeline and documents.

Would you like me to:
A) Draft that email to Maria summarizing these questions and asking for a prep session?  
B) Create a one-page timeline template you can fill in and send to her?  
C) Help you practice answers to the likely questions (I can generate practice Q&A you can rehearse)?

Tell me which option (A, B, C) or other help you prefer, and please confirm whether these are separate cases or connected — and the upcoming deadline.

