- Logistics: where/when to appear, expected length of hearing, need for interpreter, courtroom conduct rules.
- Confidentiality: what can/can’t I say publicly or on social media while this is pending?

2) Likely questions from judge or opposing counsel — what to prepare and short answer guidance
(Use short, truthful, first‑person answers. Pause and think. If you don’t know, say you don’t know rather than guessing. Ask to confer with counsel if needed.)

If this is an eviction/landlord-tenant hearing:
- “Are you the tenant/occupant?” — Short: “Yes, I am the tenant.”
- “Do you have a lease? When did it start/end?” — Bring lease; answer dates clearly.
- “Have you received any notices (pay rent or quit, termination)?” — Bring copies and say when you were served.
- “Have you paid rent? When was the last payment?” — Bring bank records/receipts.
- “Why haven’t you vacated?” — Be concise: state the reason (repair disputes, inability to pay, disputing notice) and refer to evidence.
- “Have you tried to resolve this with the landlord?” — Say what you did and provide messages/emails.

If custody or family‑related questions arise:
- “What is the current living arrangement for the child?” — Brief factual description.
- “Who has primary custody or who has been the primary caregiver?” — Brief facts and dates.
- “Are there any safety or abuse concerns?” — If yes, be forthright and provide documentation; if no, say so clearly.
- “What outcome are you asking the court to order?” — State your request succinctly and why it’s in the child’s best interest.

If immigration consequences are involved:
- “What is your current immigration status?” — Provide the exact status or say you are consulting counsel.
- “Are you aware this outcome could affect your immigration case?” — Indicate you are working with counsel to understand implications.

Cross-examination style questions to prepare for:
- Questions that press inconsistencies (dates, locations, amounts). Review your timeline and documents to keep answers consistent.
- Questions about motives or omissions. Prepare short explanations without volunteering extra info.

3) Practical preparation checklist (documents, logistics, and courtroom behavior)
Documents to gather and bring (organized, with copies for the court/opponent and your lawyer):
- Complaint, notice, service papers, discovery produced
- Lease, rent ledger, receipts, bank statements, proof of payments
- Copies of notices served or received, repair requests, tenant/landlord communications (texts, emails)
- Photos, videos, repair estimates, inspection reports
- Witness contact info and written statements/declarations
- Identification and any immigration documents (if relevant)
- Any court orders or prior judgments relating to custody or tenancy

Prepare with your attorney:
- A short factual timeline (one‑page) you can memorize and hand to the lawyer.
- Mock Q&A: do at least one run with direct and cross-exam style questions.
- Mark and index exhibits so they’re easy to find during the hearing.

Courtroom conduct and testimony tips:
- Tell the truth; be concise; answer only what is asked.
- Use “I” statements: “I received,” “I paid,” “I saw” rather than generalizations.
- Don’t argue with the judge; if you disagree, say “With respect, may I explain?” or ask your lawyer to address it.
- If you don’t understand a question, ask the court to clarify: “I’m sorry, could you repeat that?”
- Do not post about the case on social media.
- If you need a break or need to consult your lawyer before answering a question, ask for it.

