General courtroom tips:
- Keep answers short, truthful, and to the point. Don’t volunteer extra information.
- If you don’t remember, say “I don’t recall” or “I need to check that” — don’t guess.
- Address the judge as “Your Honor.” Speak slowly. Pause and consult your attorney before answering anything complicated.
- If opposing counsel asks a question you haven’t discussed with your lawyer, say you’d like to consult your attorney before answering.

Eviction (landlord-tenant)
- “When did you move in?” — have lease start date ready.
- “Have you paid rent? If not, why?” — facts about payments, dates, receipts, partial payments, attempted payments.
- “Did you receive a notice to quit/pay or a termination notice?” — know the date and whether service was proper.
- “Are there habitability issues (mold, no heat, repairs)?” — have photos, repair requests, communication records.
- “Have you corresponded with the landlord about repairs or payments?” — bring texts/emails/letters.
- “Did someone else live with you or sublet?” — be factual.
Preparation: have rent records, bank statements, receipts, notices, photographs, and any city housing code complaints.

Immigration
- “What is your current immigration status?” — have documents (A-number, form receipts) ready.
- “What relief are you asking for?” — know the basis (cancellation, adjustment, asylum, bond).
- “Do you have criminal history or arrests?” — be prepared to disclose and provide details; coordinate with counsel.
- “Why do you fear return (if asylum/withholding)?” — concise, factual statements; work with counsel to craft testimony.
Preparation: birth/identity documents, prior filings, criminal records, immigration receipts, evidence supporting relief.

Custody / Family court
- “What custody/visitation arrangement are you seeking?” — be specific about schedule and reasons.
- “Why is this in the child’s best interest?” — facts about your involvement, child’s schooling, healthcare.
- “Are there allegations of abuse, neglect, substance abuse?” — be prepared with responses and corroborating evidence or to discuss the allegations with your attorney first.
- “Where has the child been living? Who provides care?” — dates, routines, childcare arrangements.
Preparation: custody orders, school records, medical records, text/email communication about the child, witness lists (teachers, pediatrician).

3) Suggested short answers and practice lines
- If asked about dates: “I was served on [date].” (If unsure: “I need to check the exact date on the paperwork.”)
- If asked about payments or missed rent: “I paid rent on [dates]. I missed payments from [date] to [date] because [brief reason].”
- If asked about habitability: “I reported [issue] on [date] by [text/email/phone]. I have photos and messages I can provide.”
- If asked about allegations in custody: “Those allegations are not accurate. I will explain with evidence through my attorney.” (Then briefly state facts, not arguments.)
- Immigration credibility: keep a concise narrative of facts that counsel will refine; avoid embellishment.

4) Document checklist to gather now
- Service documents (summons, complaint, notice): copy and date.
- Lease, rent receipts, bank statements, canceled checks.
- Written communication with landlord, co-tenant, or opposing parent (texts, emails).
- Photographs/videos with timestamps (unit condition, damage).
- Repair requests, city code complaints, professional repair invoices.
- Immigration documents: passport, I-94, prior filings, notices to appear, receipts.
- Custody-related records: custody orders, parenting plans, school, medical records, police reports (if any).
- Witness contact info and short description of what they will say.
- Any criminal record documents or police reports that may be relevant.

5) Immediate “short window” action checklist
- Confirm and note exact service date and response deadline.
- Ask your attorney to file an appearance/answer or to move for an extension if you need time.
- Preserve all evidence (don’t delete relevant messages).
- Create a one-page timeline of key events (dates, communications, payments, incidents).
- Provide your attorney with signed authorizations or documents they request promptly.
- Request a prioritized list from your attorney of what to produce in the next 48–72 hours.
- If there’s any risk of eviction occurring before immigration or custody hearings, ask about emergency stays or temporary relief.

6) Strategy / coordination questions to ask Maria Santos
- How will you coordinate with other counsel (immigration attorney, family attorney) if these matters overlap?
- What is our plan if opposing counsel seeks expedited relief or files for emergency relief in family court?
- How will we handle testimony vs declarations? Will I testify or submit declarations?
- What are the top five facts we must prove and the top five facts to avoid or mitigate?
- What messaging should I use in negotiations or settlement discussions?

7) Practice offer
