- Ask your attorney whether they will file a response, motion to extend, or a motion to dismiss. If extension is possible, request it in writing immediately.  
- Preserve all evidence (don’t delete texts/emails; preserve social media posts; save physical receipts).  
- Get any witness statements/declarations signed and notarized if possible.  
- If eviction: if paying into court will avoid emergency relief, ask whether to deposit rent or propose a remediation plan.  
- If custody: ask about temporary emergency orders (custody, restraining orders) if there is immediate danger.  
- For immigration: ensure any application deadlines are met and that counsel has copies of all prior filings and evidence.

5) Practical hearing-prep tips
- Meet with your attorney for a mock Q&A. Practice concise, truthful answers (one to two sentences). Avoid rambling.  
- Prepare a one-page summary of your position/facts and a chronology of key dates to hand to your attorney.  
- Organize copies: one binder for the court, one for opposing counsel, one for yourself/attorney. Tab key exhibits.  
- Bring originals for verification and electronic backups (USB or secure cloud link).  
- Dress conservatively, arrive early, and know exactly which courtroom and docket time.  
- Turn off your phone. Address the judge respectfully (“Your Honor”). Follow attorney instructions about when to speak.  
- If you don’t understand a question—say so and ask for clarification. If you don’t remember an exact date, say “I don’t recall the exact date” rather than guessing.  
- Don’t volunteer extra information. Answer the question asked. If attorney instructs you not to answer without counsel present, follow that guidance.  
