Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Quick note before the content: I attempted to forward this to your legal-processing address (vince2000@gmail.com) but the system blocked that action. I can’t send emails from here, so below I’ll give you a ready-to-copy package you can email to Maria Santos (and/or to Vince) and use to prepare for the next steps.

Disclaimer: I’m not your attorney. This is organizational and preparatory assistance to help you collect facts, ask focused questions, and prepare for hearings. Confirm all legal strategy and filings with Maria Santos before acting.

1) High-priority questions to ask Maria Santos (copyable into an email)
- What are the exact deadlines I must meet now? (deadline to respond, time to file motions, date by which I must appear or request a continuance)
- What is our overall strategy for the eviction/immigration/custody interactions? (defensive strategy, settlement strategy, what to concede vs fight)
- Has the landlord filed the complaint in Denver County Court (or another court)? What is the case number and judge assigned?
- What are the immediate risks if I miss the response deadline (default judgment, writ of restitution, immigration consequences)?
- Do we need to file an Answer, Motion to Dismiss, or Request for Continuance right now? Can you file it on my behalf? If so, by when?
- Are there any immigration consequences from the eviction or from facts likely to be presented? If so, what are they and how will we address them in immigration court?
- How does the custody case (opposing parent) interact with eviction or immigration proceedings? Should we notify the family/custody attorney or file a protective pleading?
- Who will attend the hearing(s)? Will you represent me in court, or do I need to appear in person/pro se?
- Which witnesses should we plan to call or get affidavits from? Will we need subpoenas for documents or witnesses?
- What documents and evidence do you need from me immediately to prepare our response and any motions? (ask for a prioritized list)
- Should we file any emergency motions (stay, continuance, motion to seal, protective order)? If so, what info do you need to draft them now?
- What will opposing counsel likely ask and how would you prefer I handle testimony (testify vs not)?
- What is the plan if the judge sets a hearing/trial date — trial timeline, discovery deadlines, pretrial conference dates?
- Cost and timeline: expected fees and timeline to resolution for the strategy you recommend.

2) Likely questions a judge may ask at initial hearing and concise ways to respond
(Answer only factual, concise statements. Pause and consult counsel if unsure.)

- Judge: “Are you the defendant/respondent? Did you receive service?”
  - Short answer: “Yes, Your Honor. I was served on [date].” (Have the proof of service date ready.)

- Judge: “What is your current address and contact?”
  - Short answer: Give current address and phone/email.

- Judge: “Do you dispute service or jurisdiction?”
  - Short answer: “No, Your Honor” or “Yes — I dispute service because [one short factual reason].” (Explain only if you have documentary support.)

- Judge: For eviction: “What is the basis of the landlord’s claim (nonpayment, holdover, nuisance)?” / “Do you owe rent?”
  - Short answer: “The landlord claims [nonpayment/holdover]. My position is [I paid rent / I have a dispute because of repair/retaliation/etc.]. Here is the evidence.” (Have receipts and date-specific facts.)

- Judge: “Were you given a notice to quit/notice to pay or vacate? When?”
  - Short answer: “Yes — I received a [type of notice] on [date].” (Have the notice.)

- Judge: “Have you made attempts to resolve this with the landlord?”
  - Short answer: “Yes. I contacted them on [dates], and here are messages/receipts.” or “No, Your Honor.”

- Judge: “Do you request more time to respond/relocate?”
  - Short answer: “Yes, Your Honor. I request [X] days to respond/seek counsel/obtain housing.” (If requesting continuance, specify reason concisely: needing counsel, translation, urgent documents.)

- Judge: “Are there children or safety concerns at the property?”
  - Short answer: State facts succinctly: “Yes, there are children ages X and Y” or “There are safety concerns: [police reports, repairs, mold — dates].”

- Immigration court judge (if asked about status or A-file items): “Are you represented? Do you understand the charges/relief being sought?”
  - Short answer: “Yes, I am represented by Ms. Santos” (or “No, I’m representing myself”); “I understand” or “I need time to consult counsel.”

Note: Always speak truthfully and briefly. If you don’t know or need counsel’s advice, say “I need to consult my attorney before answering.”

3) Questions opposing counsel is likely to ask and how to prepare
Opposing counsel will try to establish chronology, credibility, and legal basis for relief. Prepare documentary support for each.

Common opposing counsel lines and prep:
- “When did you last pay rent? Provide receipts.”
  - Prep: Rent ledger, bank transfers, checks, cash receipts, money order copies.

- “Did you receive notice to vacate/notice to pay? When?”
  - Prep: Certified mail receipts; the actual notice; text/email.

- “Why did you not vacate / why are you withholding rent?”
  - Prep: Repair requests, landlord’s refusal, habitability issues, evidence (photos, repair tickets, municipal complaints).

- “Have you been evicted before or been subject to other orders?”
  - Prep: Disclosure of prior evictions/records if any; be prepared to explain with dates.

- “Who else lives at the property? Are there children? Any safety issues?”
  - Prep: Identification documents, custody orders, school records, police reports.

- For immigration/custody overlap: “Are you providing false information or non-custodial arrangements?”
  - Prep: Custody orders, court filings, affidavits showing parental responsibilities and residence.

How to answer:
- Prepare short factual answers and point to documentary evidence.
- Don’t speculate or volunteer extra facts.
- Ask for breaks to consult your lawyer if opposing counsel asks for legal interpretation.

4) Checklist of documents and evidence to gather immediately
Bring originals and make copies (give your lawyer copies, keep originals):

Case basics:
- Complaint, summons, Notice to Appear (NTA) or immigration charging documents, case numbers, proof of service.
- Any court filings you’ve already made.

Proof of identity and status:
- Government ID, immigration documents (I-94, I-797, NTA, A-file number, any work authorizations), social security card (if relevant).

Housing and payment evidence:
- Lease or rental agreement (signed)
- Rent ledger (month-by-month)
- Bank statements, cancelled checks, money orders, receipts showing payments and dates
- Payment apps screenshots (Venmo/PayPal) and landlord acknowledgements
- Security deposit receipt and move-in/move-out inventory

Communications:
- Texts, emails, written notices, repair requests, landlord notices to pay/vacate
- Certified mail tracking numbers, proof of service

Habitability and safety:
- Photos/videos of unit (with dates), repair invoices, municipal code violations, police reports, 311/311-equivalent complaints
- Witness statements or affidavits (neighbors, roommates, contractors)

Discovery & litigation materials:
- Discovery responses, interrogatories, deposition transcripts (if any)
- Documents produced by landlord
- Any settlement offers or mediation correspondence

Custody/Family:
- Current custody orders, parenting plans, court filings, child support orders, school or medical records showing child’s address

Immigration:
- Copies of any prior immigration filings, counsel contact for immigration, country documents, criminal records (if any)

