While you confirm, here’s a prioritized, practical checklist and suggested questions to bring to Maria Santos plus likely questions the judge or opposing counsel may ask. You can use these to prepare answers and a short script for the hearing.

Immediate priorities (do these now)
- Confirm the deadline: ask Maria the exact date/time by which you must file any response or pleading. Missing it can have serious consequences.
- Ask whether she will file the response for you and whether you need to sign anything.
- Ask for an emergency extension/motion for more time if you won’t meet the deadline.
- Preserve evidence: don’t delete messages, lease papers, texts, photos, receipts, or anything related to the dispute.
- Avoid substantive discussion about the case with the opposing party without your lawyer present.

Questions to ask Maria Santos (general / essential)
- What is the immediate deadline I must meet and what will you file on my behalf?
- What type of hearing is this (status, preliminary, bench trial, merits)? What is the likely timeline to the final hearing?
- What are the legal claims and defenses we should be raising?
- What outcome options should I expect (dismissal, settlement, default judgment, eviction, custody order, immigration consequence)?
- What is your recommended strategy? (Defend, seek continuance, mediation, settlement.)
- Which facts or documents are most important for my case?
- Who should we identify as witnesses and what will they testify to?
- Do we need expert testimony (e.g., appraiser, social worker, immigration expert)?
- What motions might the other side file? What motions should we consider (motion to dismiss, motion to compel, motion for continuance, motion in limine)?
- Are there any immigration or criminal consequences I should be concerned about from this case?
- How will custody / family issues affect my immigration or eviction case (if relevant)?
- What will happen at the hearing step-by-step? How long will it take and what should I say?
- What behavior or statements should I avoid (especially anything that could hurt credibility or immigration status)?
- How should I prepare and practice for direct testimony and cross-examination?
- Who is my contact for day-of logistics and how do I reach you if something urgent comes up?

Case-specific questions and likely questions you should prepare to answer

A. Landlord–Tenant/Eviction (Denver County)
Likely questions from judge/opposing counsel:
- Did you receive the notice to quit or demand? When?
- Have you paid rent? If not, why not? Which months are unpaid?
- Do you have a written lease? What are the lease terms?
- Have you attempted to cure the issue (paid, repaired, moved)?
- Are there habitability issues (mold, heat, plumbing)? Did you notify landlord in writing?
- Have you been provided with proper notices required by Colorado law?
- Who lives at the property? Are there other tenants or occupants?
- Have you refused entry to landlord or violated lease terms?
- Do you have documentation (receipts, photos, texts) to support your position?

B. Custody/Family Court (if “opposing parent” is involved)
Likely questions:
- What is your relationship to the child? Custody/parenting time requested?
- What is your proposed parenting plan or schedule?
- Are there safety concerns (domestic violence, substance abuse) and what evidence supports them?
- What is the child’s primary residence and who provides care on a day-to-day basis?
- How is the child doing academically, medically, emotionally? Any pending concerns?
- Have you attempted mediation or parent coordination?
- Are there witnesses (teachers, doctors, counselors) who can speak to the child’s best interest?
- What specific parenting responsibilities do you want the court to order?

C. Immigration Court (if proceedings are pending)
Likely questions:
- What is your current immigration status and how were you served?
- Have you or a family member been convicted of any crimes?
- What is the basis of your claim (asylum, relief, cancellation, adjustment)?
- Do you have evidence of fear of return, persecution, or hardship?
- How do any custody or criminal issues intersect with immigration claims?
- Have you previously applied for immigration benefits or been deported?
- Are there witnesses or corroborating evidence for your claim?

Documents and evidence checklist to bring / assemble
- Court papers and notices (summons, complaint, notice to quit, immigration charging documents)
- Lease or rental agreement and correspondence with landlord
- Receipts for rent paid, bank records, canceled checks
- Photographs (dates stamped if possible) of property condition or incidents
- Texts, emails, social-media messages related to the dispute
- Police reports, medical records, CPS or school records (custody)
- Identification documents, immigration paperwork (A-number, passports, prior filings)
- Witness names, contact info, written statements or declarations
- Any discovery responses you received and notes about missing/late discovery

How the judge typically conducts the hearing and preparation tips
- Judges want concise, factual answers. Practice short, truthful statements that directly answer the question.
- Prepare a one-paragraph summary (30–60 seconds) of your position/facts for the judge if asked to summarize.
- Don’t volunteer extra details — answer the question asked, then stop.
- If you don’t know or don’t remember, say so rather than guessing.
- Bring at least 2 copies of every document (one for the judge, one for opposing counsel) organized and tabbed.
- Rehearse with counsel: practice direct testimony and handle common cross-exam questions.
- Discuss with Maria whether you should testify. If so, review credibility issues and prior inconsistent statements.
- Ask about courtroom attire and arrival time; confirm remote hearing link if it’s virtual.

Practical preparation tasks you can ask Maria to do or help with
- Draft and file the required response/answer or motion before the deadline.
- Move for continuance if more time is needed to prepare.
- Exchange exhibits and witness lists; handle any outstanding discovery.
- Prepare witness declarations/affidavits.
- Prepare a hearing outline and a short oral argument script you can memorize.
- Discuss settlement options and whether mediation would be appropriate.

Sample short script you can use to ask Maria (I can draft an email for you)
