I can help—this is a lot to prepare for. First, a quick clarification so I tailor the questions correctly:

- Are these all parts of the same matter (eviction + immigration + custody), or are they separate cases you’re facing at once?  
- Do you already have a deadline (date) to file a response or an upcoming hearing date?  
- Do you want: (A) a list of suggested questions to ask your attorney, (B) likely questions the judge/opposing counsel may ask and short practice answers, or (C) a drafted email to send to Maria Santos now? (I can do any or all.)

Below is a focused set of things to ask your attorney plus likely court/counsel questions, document checklist, and prep tips. Use or copy whichever parts you want.

Suggested questions to ask your attorney (grouped by topic)
- Case status and deadlines
  - What exactly must I file and by what date? (Answer, motion, other response?)
  - When will we likely receive a hearing date? Can we request more time if needed?
  - Are any immediate deadlines coming up I might be missing?

- Strategy and likely outcomes
  - What is our overall strategy for this stage (settlement, motion to dismiss, full hearing)?
  - What are the strengths and weaknesses of our case?
  - What is the judge likely to focus on in an eviction/custody/immigration context?

- Evidence and witnesses
  - Which documents and evidence are most important for the hearing?
  - Do we need witness declarations or will witnesses testify in person?
  - Should we subpoena any documents or witnesses now?

- Procedure and filings
  - Should we file any prehearing motions (e.g., motion to continue, exclude evidence, protective order)?
  - How do we authenticate documents and exhibit lists for the hearing?
  - What is the format for exhibits and how many copies are needed?

- Interacting with opposing counsel
  - What should I say or avoid saying if opposing counsel contacts me?
  - Can we negotiate a settlement or protective parenting plan before the hearing? What parameters should I accept/refuse?

- Immigration-specific (if immigration court is involved)
  - Could any action in the eviction/custody matter affect my immigration status?
  - Are there forms or filings I should prepare for immigration court or to preserve relief?

- Custody-specific (if the other parent is involved)
  - What evidence best supports my parenting/time with the child?
  - Will a custody evaluator or GAL be appointed? How to prepare?

- Risks, costs, and timeline
  - What are the realistic best-case and worst-case outcomes?
  - What are anticipated costs (court fees, expert fees, travel) and timeline to resolution?
  - If we lose at hearing, what are our appeal or post-judgment options?

- Communication and next steps
  - How and when should I contact you with new information or questions?
  - What do you need from me in the next 48–72 hours?

Likely questions the judge or opposing counsel may ask (and how to prepare short answers)
Note: keep answers factual, concise, and supported by documents.

Eviction-related questions
- Did you receive the notice(s)? When and how were you served? (Have proof of service.)
- Have you paid rent, and if not, why? (Bring rent ledger, receipts, bank records.)
- Did you notify the landlord of needed repairs? What was the response? (Show emails/texts/work orders.)
- Do you have a lease? What are key lease terms (term length, rent amount, late fees)?

Custody-related questions
- What is your current living situation? Who lives with you? (Provide address and household details.)
- What is the child’s routine and where do they attend school/medical care?
- Are there any safety concerns for the child? (Be specific; have records if applicable.)
- What are you asking the court to order (parenting time, decision-making) and why?

Immigration-related questions (if relevant)
- What is your current immigration status and what relief are you seeking?
- Have you been arrested or convicted of any offenses? (Be truthful; bring relevant records.)
- Are there pending removal charges or previous immigration proceedings?

General courtroom behavior and credibility questions
- Are there inconsistencies in your prior statements? (Review prior filings and emails; note any differences and how to explain them.)
- Can you identify key witnesses and what they will testify to?
- Are there documents that contradict your position? (Be prepared to address them honestly.)

Document checklist (bring originals + copies where possible)
- Lease or rental agreement
- Notices (pay or quit, notice to vacate) and proofs of service
- Rent payment history: receipts, bank transfers, ledger
- Written communications with landlord/opposing parent (emails, texts)
- Repair requests and response records (photos, invoices)
- Police reports, incident reports, or protective orders (if any)
- Child records: school records, medical records, daycare info
- Immigration documents: passports, I-94, receipts, prior immigration filings
- Discovery responses and served documents from other side
- Witness contact list with short summary of expected testimony

Practical preparation & testimony tips
- Review your timeline: know dates, amounts, and key events—don’t guess.
- Practice concise answers: one- or two-sentence responses; don’t volunteer extra information.
- If you don’t know or don’t remember, say so (“I don’t recall” or “I need to check the record”), rather than guessing.
- Be calm, respectful, and dress conservatively for court.
- Prepare short direct examination points for your witnesses; prepare your testimony in bullet points.
- Ask your attorney to role-play cross-examination with you.

Questions your attorney should prepare you to answer directly (practice list)
- “How much rent did you pay and when?” (Bring exact receipts.)
- “When were you served with the notice?” (Cite proof of service.)
- “Why haven’t you complied with the notice?” (Factual, brief explanation.)
- “Where does the child sleep/study/receive medical care?” (Specific locations and schedules.)
- “Have you ever been convicted of ____?” (Answer truthfully and refer to documents.)

Quick checklist of immediate actions to discuss with Maria Santos
- Confirm any filing deadlines and whether an Answer must be filed now.
- Decide whether to request a continuance to prepare.
- Determine critical exhibits and any necessary witness declarations/subpoenas.
- Confirm communication protocol if opposing counsel contacts you.
- Schedule time to review documents and to role-play testimony with counsel.

Want me to draft a concise email to Maria Santos now?
I can draft an email you can send to Maria that includes:
- A short case summary,
- The urgent questions (deadlines, filing needs, strategy),
- A request for a meeting or phone call and what you’ll bring.

If you want that, tell me:
- Do you want it short (one paragraph) or detailed (includes the full checklist above)?
- Do you want to send it now or save it as a draft?

Would you like me to draft the email to Maria Santos now? If so, tell me whether to keep it short or detailed and whether to include any dates or additional specifics.

