I tried to send your message to vince2000@gmail.com per your request, but sending is blocked in this session. I can still help right away by organizing the questions, drafting an email you can send, and preparing a short hearing-prep script or role‑play. Below is a clear, copy‑pasteable plan you can use with your attorney and to practice.

Quick clarifying question before we go further
- Are the eviction, immigration, and custody matters all part of a single dispute or are they separate cases that just overlap in timing? (This changes strategy and what to ask.)

Suggested questions to ask Maria Santos (grouped by topic)
1) Case overview & strategy
- What is our overall strategy for each matter (eviction, immigration proceedings, custody)? Are we coordinating strategy across them?
- What are the realistic outcomes and chances for each possibility?
- Who will handle which hearings or filings (you or other counsel)?

2) Deadlines & immediate steps
- What exact deadlines do I have now (response to service, filing deadlines, motions)?
- What happens if we miss a deadline? Can we get an extension or file a late response?
- Are there emergency or temporary relief options we should consider now?

3) Evidence & discovery
- What specific documents and evidence do you need from me right away?
- Do we need witness statements? Who should be witnesses?
- How will we use the discovery already completed? Any follow-up discovery requests needed?

4) Hearing preparation & logistics
- How will the hearing be structured (time limits, live testimony, exhibits)?
- Which exhibits should I prepare and bring copies of?
- Will you prepare a brief or witness outline? Can I get a copy to review?

5) Questions the judge might ask
- What basic facts will the judge expect me to state (my relationship to the child, tenancy details, timeline of events)?
- What are the judge’s likely concerns in custody vs. eviction vs. immigration contexts?

6) Questions opposing counsel might ask / cross-examination
- What are the likely lines of attack from opposing counsel?
- How should I respond to aggressive questioning or questions about immigration status or prior records?

7) Immigration-specific concerns
- Could the eviction or custody situation affect my immigration case (e.g., credibility, removability, relief eligibility)?
- Should we inform the immigration court about the other proceedings? Will that help or hurt?
- Are there forms or evidence (I-94, notices, prior filings) I must provide now?

8) Custody-specific concerns
- What documentation shows stable housing, parenting time, or ability to care for the child?
- If the other parent raises allegations, how will we rebut them? Do we need third-party documentation (school records, medical records)?

9) Settlement & negotiation
- Are settlement or mediation options available? What are realistic settlement terms?
- If we negotiate, what are your recommended bottom lines?

10) Practical & courtroom behavior
- How should I dress and behave in court? What should I say when the judge asks questions?
- Are phones allowed? Where to sit? When to speak?

Documents checklist to gather now
- Lease, rental agreement, notices (eviction notices, 3-day/30-day notices)
- Proof of service and court papers you were served with
- Rent payment records, receipts, bank records, electronic payments
- Correspondence with landlord, other parent, or opposing counsel (email, text)
- Discovery responses and any exhibits produced
- Child-related records (birth certificate, school records, medical records, daycare)
- Immigration documents (I‑94, prior filings, receipts, notices to appear)
- IDs, utility bills, photos of the property, repair records
- Any police reports or protective orders (if relevant)

How the judge/opposing counsel might question you — sample questions and short model answers
(Keep answers very short, factual, and only answer what’s asked. Don’t volunteer extra details.)

Judge: “Can you state your full name and relationship to the child?”
- Model: “My name is [Full Name]. I am the child’s [mother/father/guardian].”

Judge: “What is the current living situation?”
- Model: “I live at [address]. I have lived there since [date].” (If that’s disputed, say “I believe I moved in on…” and let counsel handle specifics.)

Judge: “Why are you being evicted?”
- Model: “The landlord served an eviction notice on [date] for [nonpayment/lease violation]. I have [paid partial amounts/paid on these dates/have a dispute about condition].”

Opposing counsel: “Isn’t it true that you did X on [date]?”
- Model: “No, that did not happen,” or “I don’t recall that,” then refer to evidence: “My records show…” — coordinate with attorney before elaborating.

Important tips for answers and conduct
- Be concise, truthful, and calm. Pause before answering if you need to think.
- If you don’t know or don’t remember, say so — do not guess.
- Avoid discussing immigration status or other sensitive facts unless attorney advises it’s necessary.
- Don’t sign anything or speak to the other side about settlement without your lawyer’s permission.

Sample email you can copy/paste and send to Maria Santos
Subject: Preparation + questions — discovery complete, waiting for hearing date

Hi Maria —

Discovery is complete and I’ve been served; we are waiting for the hearing date. I want to be prepared and would like to confirm deadlines and next steps. Below are items and questions I’d like your guidance on:

1) Confirm case status and deadlines:
- What immediate deadlines do I have (response to service, filings)?
- Are there extensions or emergency motions we should consider?

2) Evidence & documents I should gather (please confirm):
- Lease, notices, rent records, photos, communications with landlord/other parent
- Child-related documents (birth certificate, school/medical records)
- Immigration documents (I-94, prior filings, notices)

3) Hearing logistics:
- How is the hearing structured? Time allotment, exhibits, witness testimony?
- Will you prepare a pre-hearing brief or outline? Can I review it?

4) Expected questions & preparation:
- What questions might the judge or opposing counsel ask in the eviction, custody, and immigration contexts?
- Any advice about what I should say or avoid saying in court?

5) Strategy & coordination:
- How will we coordinate our approach across the eviction, custody, and immigration proceedings?
- Should we consider settlement or mediation at this stage?

Please let me know a good time to meet or speak for a detailed prep session. I can bring the documents listed above.

Thank you,
[Your full name]
[Phone number]

Want a short hearing script or role‑play?
- I can prepare a one-page “cheat sheet” with the three-minute summary for the judge, answers to the 10 most likely judge questions, and a 10-minute role-play script to practice with. Would you like that?

Next steps I can do for you now
- Produce the one-page hearing script
- Role‑play likely Q&A with you (I’ll ask and you answer)
- Create a printable checklist of documents and a timeline you can bring to your attorney

Which would you like me to prepare next? Also confirm whether the three matters are connected so I can tailor the questions and script precisely.

