First, quick note: I tried to forward your message automatically but a system restriction blocked that. I can still help you here — and, if you want, I can draft an email or a one-page checklist you can send to Maria Santos (you would then send it yourself).

Below I’ve organized practical things to ask your attorney, likely questions a judge or opposing counsel might ask in each area (eviction, immigration, custody), what to prepare and bring to the hearing, and short, practice-style answers you can use. I’ll finish by offering to draft a concise email to your lawyer with these points.

Important: I’m not your lawyer. Use these suggestions to prepare questions and practice with Maria Santos.

1) Top questions to ask your attorney right now
- What is the exact deadline to file any response or motion? What counts as “filed” and how do we do it (e-file, mail, in-person)?
- Was service valid? Can we challenge service or get additional time?
- What is the next likely court date and expected procedure at that hearing?
- What are the realistic best-case and worst-case outcomes for this hearing?
- What motions should we consider now (motion to dismiss, continuance, motion to exclude evidence, protective order, emergency relief)?
- What evidence or documentation should I gather and bring to the hearing?
- Who should testify for us (me, witnesses, experts)? How will witnesses be subpoenaed if needed?
- Are there immigration consequences I should worry about from this eviction/custody matter? How will this fit with the immigration proceeding?
- Is there any strategy you recommend for settlement or mediation before the hearing?
- What will opposing counsel likely ask me, and how should I answer?
- How should I behave in court (testimony style, demeanor, clothing, cell phone rules)?
- What are the costs/fees for more work (motions, more discovery, additional hearings)?
- If I get an adverse ruling, what are the appeal options and timelines?
- Any conflicts between the eviction, custody, and immigration cases we should coordinate?

2) Likely questions from judge or opposing counsel — and short answers to practice
General guidance: keep answers short, factual, and only answer the question you’re asked. If you don’t know, say “I don’t know” or “I don’t remember” rather than guessing. Don’t volunteer extra information.

Eviction-related likely questions
- “Why are you not current on rent?” — Brief factual answer (e.g., “I fell behind because of X; I paid $X on [date]; I’ve been looking for assistance; here are records.”)
- “When did you receive the notice to quit/notice to pay?” — Give exact date or approximate if unsure; refer to the document.
- “Did you sign a lease? What are the key terms?” — State yes/no, lease dates, rent amount, and any agreed modifications.
- “Did you communicate with the landlord about the issue?” — Say yes/no, briefly summarize and offer to produce emails/texts.
- “Do you have proof of payments or receipts?” — Say yes and point to bank statements, receipts, rent ledger.

Immigration-related likely questions (if relevant to the hearing or separate immigration hearing)
- “What is your current immigration status?” — State the status simply (e.g., “I have [status], valid until [date]”).
- “Are you represented in immigration court?” — Say yes and give attorney name; confirm whether they will appear.
- “Are you asking for any stay or relief based on these proceedings?” — Say you are following counsel’s advice; request the immigration-specific counsel answer if they ask technical relief questions.

Custody-related likely questions
- “What is your relationship with the child?” — State relationship and living arrangements factually.
- “Who has been the primary caregiver?” — Briefly state the facts (days, responsibilities).
- “Why are you seeking / opposing the requested custody/visitation?” — Answer with child-focused facts: stability, safety, child’s best interest.
- “Are there any safety concerns or domestic violence history?” — If relevant, say yes and be prepared to give dates and evidence; if not, say no.
- “What is your proposed parenting time plan?” — Have a clear, feasible proposal ready.

Cross-cutting judicial questions
- “Can you summarize the timeline of events?” — Have a short timeline: dates and one-sentence descriptions.
- “Do you have documents to support that?” — Say yes and identify exhibits: lease, notices, emails, bank statements, police reports, school records.
- “Is there anything you want the court to know?” — Keep this concise and focused on facts bearing on the issue.

3) What to prepare and bring (documents and evidence checklist)
- Court papers served to you (complaint/summons/notice of hearing) and proof of service.
- Lease or rental agreement, any amendments, and landlord communications.
- Rent payment records: bank statements, canceled checks, money order receipts, rent ledgers.
- Notices: notice to pay/quit, notices of termination, any cure letters.
- Correspondence: emails, texts, written messages between you and landlord or other parent.
- Discovery responses you provided and what you received from opposition.
- Witness list and contact info (neighbors, co-residents, childcare providers, landlords).
- Photos, videos, repair requests, police or incident reports.
- For custody: school records, medical records, childcare receipts, schedules showing who cares for the child.
- For immigration: I-94, receipt notices, prior filings, immigration counsel contact.
- Identification and any court orders from related cases.
- A one-page timeline of events you can use as a reference.
- Copies for judge and opposing counsel (check local rules about exhibits—bring extra sets).

4) Evidence and testimony prep
- Mark exhibits clearly, paginate, and prepare an exhibit index.
- Review discovery responses and confirm nothing was missed or needs supplementing.
- Meet with Maria to role-play questions and practice short answers under pressure.
- Identify weak points and how to address them honestly (e.g., “I missed rent because…” with corroborating documents).
- Prepare witness statements and confirm availability for hearing.

