--- AGENT RESPONSE (FULL) ---
Length: 6264 chars
Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — this is a good place to start. Below is a focused checklist you can use when you talk with Maria Santos, plus a list of likely questions the judge or opposing counsel might ask and how to prepare answers. If you want, I can also draft an email to Maria with these items or a shorter meeting request.

A. Immediate procedural items (ask your attorney first)
- What is the exact deadline to file any response (answer, motion, or declaration)? Is that deadline calendared?
- Were there any required forms or affidavits I must file now? (Identify exact documents and where to file.)
- Can we request an extension if I need more time? If so, how and when should that be done?
- Has service been confirmed (proof of service on file)? Do we need to file/obtain proof?
- What is the likely timeline from now to hearing (motion schedule, pre-hearing conferences, hearing type)?

B. Case posture & strategy (questions to ask your attorney)
- How does the eviction/landlord-tenant claim intersect with the custody/immigration issues (if at all)? Are there risks to my immigration case from this eviction or related filings?
- What is our legal theory / defense? (e.g., procedural defects, defenses to eviction, tenancy facts, evidence of payment/repairs)
- What are the strengths and weaknesses of our position?
- What remedies or outcomes should I realistically expect (dismissal, settlement, delay, conditional orders)?
- Are there motions we should file before the hearing (motion to dismiss, motion for continuance, motions in limine)?
- Should we prepare a written declaration or timeline for the judge? What should it include?

C. Evidence & documents to gather
Ask attorney which of the following to collect and bring to your meeting/hearing:
- Lease or rental agreement, receipts of rent payments (bank records, Venmo, checks)
- Notices (eviction notices, pay-or-quit, termination letters)
- Communication records with landlord or opposing party (texts, emails, messages)
- Repair requests, photos/videos of property condition, receipts for repairs
- Police reports or 911 calls (if relevant)
- Any court documents or filings in the custody case that might be relevant
- Immigration-related documents that could be affected (copies only if attorney says)
- Witness names and contact info; brief summary of what each would testify to
- Proof of service received and the documents you were served with

D. Questions the judge might ask (and how to prepare answers)
- “Have you been properly served?” — Bring proof of receipt; if you disagree, say so respectfully and provide evidence.
- “What relief are you asking the court to grant?” — Know your requested outcome (dismissal, time to cure, stay).
- “When did you last pay rent / what is your payment history?” — Have dates and records ready.
- “What attempts have been made to resolve this?” — List communications, offers, repairs, or payments.
- “Are there any pending related cases (custody/immigration)?” — Be honest and brief; ask attorney how much to disclose.
- “Do you understand the consequences if you do not comply?” — If there are immigration or custody consequences, ask your attorney how to answer this and whether to defer to counsel.

E. Questions opposing counsel might ask at deposition/at hearing (prepare concise answers)
- Details about dates, payments, communications — have exact dates and documentary backup.
- Questions aimed at credibility (contradictions) — review your statement and be consistent.
- Questions trying to show you were notified but didn’t respond — have proof of communications or reasons.
- Questions about other disputes (custody) — consult Maria about whether and how to answer; limit unnecessary detail.

F. Witnesses & testimony
- Which witnesses should we call? What will each witness say?
- Do we need witness declarations or affidavits filed in advance?
- Should any witnesses be prepared for cross-examination (practice with attorney)?

G. Settlement & negotiation
- When is settlement appropriate? What minimum terms are acceptable?
- Can we negotiate to delay eviction to avoid immigration/custody impacts?
- Is mediation or stipulation to a timeline possible?

H. Immigration-specific concerns (ask plainly)
- Could an eviction or judgment be used against me in immigration proceedings?
- Should any filings be kept sealed or confidential to avoid immigration/custody use?
