Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — this is an important moment to get organized. First, quick clarification before I make a focused list: are you preparing for the landlord‑tenant eviction hearing, the custody matter, the immigration court proceeding, or some combination of these? (You mentioned all three — tell me which one you want to focus on first.)

Below is a comprehensive, practical set of questions and prep items to take to Maria Santos, plus likely questions a judge or opposing counsel may ask in each type of hearing. You can use this as a checklist or I can draft an email to Maria listing these questions for Maria.

Important: I’m not a lawyer. Use these questions to guide your conversation with Maria Santos — she will give the legal advice tailored to your case and jurisdiction (Denver County, Colorado).

Questions to ask your attorney (general / case management)
- What is the current status of the case and exact next deadlines/hearing date(s)? What is the deadline to file any response or motion? What happens if we miss it?
- Which court/department and judge will handle the hearing? Are hearings in person or remote?
- What is the scope of the hearing (pretrial status, merits hearing, temporary orders, removal/eviction, custody hearing, master calendar, merits)?
- What is our legal theory and the main facts we will emphasize?
- What outcome(s) are realistic and what are the risks?
- What documents or proof do you need from me and by when?
- Do we need witness statements, affidavits, or expert reports? If so, what specifically and by when do they need to be disclosed?
- Are there any motions we should file now (e.g., continuance, motion to dismiss, motion in limine, emergency relief)?
- Are settlement/mediation options viable right now? Pros/cons of trying to settle before the hearing?
- If opposing counsel asks to meet, negotiate, or propose stipulations, how should I respond?
- What are immediate tactical items I should do this week?

Questions about what the judge or opposing counsel may ask (how to prepare answers)
- Short procedural questions: “Have you been served?” “Do you understand the allegations?” “Are you asking for more time?” Be prepared to answer yes/no and ask for time to consult counsel.
- Factual background: Be ready to briefly state your side of the story in 2–3 sentences (who you are, what happened, what you want).
- Evidence questions: “Do you have receipts, photos, communications, or bank records?” Bring originals and copies and note where each came from.
- Credibility and timeline: “When did X happen?” “Who was present?” “Did you tell anyone about this at the time?” Practice concise, chronological answers.
- Admissions/opposing statements: “Did you say X to the other parent/landlord/agent?” If you don’t recall, say so — do not guess. Ask your attorney how to handle prior statements.
- Communication history: “Have you tried to resolve this directly?” “Did you send/receive written notices or texts?” Bring that correspondence.
- Remedy and relief: “What do you want the court to order?” Be specific about the relief you seek (e.g., stay eviction, custody time, continuance, relief from removal).
- If there are safety/immigration issues: Expect questions about risk, harm, or eligibility for relief — consult attorney for careful phrasing.

Likely/typical questions by case type
- Landlord-Tenant (Eviction)
  - Why were you late on rent (if applicable)?
  - Did you receive a notice to pay or quit / cure? When?
  - Do you have proof of rent paid or offered?
  - Were repairs requested and not made? Do you have written repair requests/photos?
  - Do you have a lease? Were any lease terms violated by you or landlord?
  - Do you have witnesses or evidence showing landlord’s conduct (e.g., lockout, utilities turned off)?
  - Are you seeking more time to move or contest the eviction? Why?

- Custody / Parent vs Parent
  - What is the current custody/visitation arrangement?
  - What changes are you seeking and why (specific incidents, safety concerns, relocation, parental fitness)?
  - What is the child’s home/school routine and what’s in the child’s best interest?
  - Are there allegations of abuse, neglect, substance use, or stability concerns? What evidence supports or refutes them?
  - Who can testify about the child’s well‑being (teachers, pediatricians, counselors)?
  - Are there any protective orders or prior court orders we must address?

- Immigration Court (you mentioned immigration court)
  - What relief are you requesting (e.g., cancellation, asylum, adjustment, bond)?
  - What is your immigration status and what documents support it?
  - Do you anticipate questions about criminal history, prior removals, or eligibility bars?
  - Are there country conditions, fear of return, or hardship factors we need to document?
  - What testimony or documents will prove the elements of the relief?

Practical evidence & organization checklist
- Originals and copies of: lease, notices, service papers, pleadings, discovery responses, emails/texts, receipts, bank statements, medical records, school records, police reports.
- A one‑page timeline of key dates (serve, notices, incidents).
- A list of witnesses with short notes on what each will say and their contact info.
- Mark and tab exhibits and provide a numbered list for counsel.
- Prepare short written statements or affidavits for key witnesses if allowed.
- Backup: email copies in a folder and printed copies in an organized binder.

Preparation for testimony / hearing behavior
- Practice a short opening statement (1–2 minutes) summarizing your position.
- Practice direct answers: keep them brief, truthful, and factual. Avoid volunteered extra facts.
- If you don’t know or don’t remember, say so rather than guessing.
- Ask your attorney to role‑play cross-examination and practice responses to tough questions.
- Dress appropriately and arrive early; bring ID and case number.
- Ask your lawyer about courtroom decorum and when/how you can speak.

Questions to ask about discovery that just ended
- Was anything late or withheld by the other side? Should we file a motion to compel or sanctions?
- Are there gaps in their production that we can use at hearing?
- Do we need to supplement our disclosures or add exhibits/witnesses now?
- Are there privilege or confidentiality issues to raise?

If you want, I can:
- Draft an email to Maria Santos listing these questions and asking for a meeting (tell me whether you want it sent or saved as a draft).
- Create a 1‑page timeline and exhibit checklist if you give me dates and documents.
- Role‑play possible opposing counsel questions with suggested concise answers.

Next step for me: tell me which case to focus on (eviction, custody, immigration, or all), and whether you want me to draft a message to Maria Santos that you can send or save as a draft.

