I can help with that. Below is a focused, practical checklist of questions to ask Maria Santos and a list of likely questions the judge or opposing counsel may ask so you can prepare. I can also draft an email to Maria listing these questions or create a mock Q&A/testimony script if you want — tell me which and I’ll draft it.

Important: I’m not your lawyer. Use these suggestions to guide your conversation with Maria; follow her legal advice.

Questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos)
- Procedural / timing
  - What is the exact deadline for filing any response or documents? Can you file on my behalf?
  - When do you expect the court to set the hearing date? How long will the hearing likely be?
  - Can we request a continuance if we need more time to prepare? What are the risks?
  - What filings or forms should I expect between now and the hearing?

- Case posture & strategy
  - What is our legal theory / defense in the eviction/custody/immigration context?
  - What are the strengths and weaknesses of our position?
  - Are there any dispositive motions we should consider before the hearing?
  - Do you recommend settlement/mediation or taking it to hearing? What are the trade-offs?

- Evidence & documents
  - What documents do you want me to gather and bring to the hearing? (e.g., lease, notices, payment records, photos, communications, repair requests, police reports, immigration documents, court orders in custody case)
  - How should documents be organized and labeled for the court and for opposing counsel?
  - Are there documents we need to serve on the opposing party or file with the court now?

- Witnesses & testimony
  - Which witnesses should we call? Who should I list on a witness list?
  - Do any witnesses need subpoenas? How do we request them?
  - Will you prepare/walk me through my testimony? Can we do a mock examination?
  - Are there witnesses who might be harmful or who opposing counsel will call? How should we handle that?

- Cross-examination & likely lines of attack
  - What questions or facts do you expect opposing counsel to emphasize?
  - How should I respond to questions about rental payments, notices, communications, or parenting arrangements?
  - What admissions should I avoid? Are there phrases or statements that could hurt our case?

- Evidence objections & admissibility
  - What common objections should we raise (hearsay, relevance, foundation)?
  - Are there any exhibits you expect the other side to try to introduce that we should pre-emptively challenge?

- Immigration & custody interplay
  - How could the eviction/custody outcome affect my immigration case or status in immigration court?
  - What should I tell immigration counsel (if different) about developments in the eviction/custody proceedings?
  - Are there protective measures (restraining order, confidentiality) we should request if custodial conflict impacts safety or immigration relief?

- Practical logistics & courtroom procedure
  - Where and when is the hearing, and how early should I arrive?
  - What should I wear and bring (IDs, copies of all exhibits)?
  - How long do you expect the hearing to last, and will you be present?
  - Will the judge ask for sworn testimony or will it be on the papers only?

- Outcomes & next steps
  - If we win or lose, what are the immediate consequences and possible appeals?
  - What is the likely timeline for enforcement (eviction writ, custody order changes)?
  - If the other parent seeks emergency relief, how quickly must we respond?

- Communication & expectations
  - How will you update me about developments? What is the best way/time to reach you?
  - Can you give me a short list of key facts/phrases to memorize for my testimony?
  - Can we schedule a prep session (mock direct and cross) before the hearing?

Likely questions judge or opposing counsel might ask (eviction / custody overlap)
- Basic facts and timeline
  - When did you move in/when did you move out (or when did the other party move out)?
  - Did you receive a written notice? When and how were you served?
  - Have you paid rent? Show dates/amounts of payments or missed payments.
  - What communications took place about the problem (email/texts/letters)? Can you produce them?

- Habitability / repairs / issues (if tenant defense)
  - When did you first report the problem (mold, heating, repairs)? Who did you notify and how?
  - What was the landlord’s response and timeframe for repairs?
  - How did the condition affect your health, safety, or ability to live there?

- Possession / termination (if landlord)
  - When did you serve the notice to quit? What was the basis for eviction (nonpayment, lease violation)?
  - Did you follow Colorado landlord-tenant notice requirements? Can you show proof?

- Credibility & relationships (custody overlap)
  - What is your relationship with the other parent? What are the custody/parenting arrangements now?
  - Are you seeking to change custody or visitation? Why?
  - Are there allegations of abuse/neglect? What evidence supports or refutes those allegations?

- Immigration-related
  - Do you understand how this proceeding could affect your immigration case?
  - Have you notified your immigration counsel?

- Damages / relief
  - What relief are you asking for (stay, dismissal, damages, custody arrangement)?
  - How did you calculate any damages or rent owed?

Practical prep suggestions
- Create a facts timeline: date-by-date list of key events, with documents attached to each date.
- Label exhibits and prepare at least three copies (for judge, opposing counsel, yourself).
- Practice concise, neutral answers (short sentences). Avoid volunteering extra information.
- Be ready to say “I don’t recall” or “I’ll need to check the document” rather than guessing.
- Do at least one mock direct and one mock cross with your attorney or with me (I can simulate opposing counsel).
- Confirm interpreter or accommodations if English isn’t your first language.
- Keep copies of everything you file and proof of service.

Would you like me to:
- Draft an email to Maria Santos listing these specific questions and requesting a mock prep session? (If yes, tell me whether to send now or save as a draft and any tone preferences.)
- Create a timeline template for you to fill with dates and documents?
- Run a mock cross-examination script (I can play opposing counsel)?

Tell me which of those you want next and any details (e.g., whether to email Maria now and her address, or what you want included).

