Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — below is a focused, practical checklist you can take to Maria Santos (or use to prepare for your hearing). It’s organized so you can prioritize what to ask now, what the judge/opposing counsel are likely to ask, how to prepare short answers, and what documents/witnesses to bring. I’m not a lawyer, so treat this as organizational and preparatory help — run anything substantive by Maria Santos before using it in court.

Quick clarification for you to confirm with your attorney
- Confirm which matters are being heard and where (state eviction case in Denver County? immigration court hearing? custody issues in family court?). Make sure you and your attorney are clear which hearing(s) are pending and whether they overlap or affect each other.

Priority questions to ask your attorney now
(ask these first at your next meeting or in an email)

Case status, timeline, and immediate deadlines
- What is the exact date, time, and location of the next hearing(s)? Is it for eviction, immigration, custody, or a combined scheduling matter?
- What deadlines do I have now (responses, filings, evidence exchange)? How long do I have to respond to the eviction papers? Can we get an extension?
- Has discovery officially closed? If so, what evidence can still be submitted and by when?

Procedural & jurisdictional
- Which court’s rules control (Denver County court rules vs. immigration court procedures)? Any local rules I must follow?
- What standard of proof applies at the hearing(s)?
- Will the hearing be in-person, remote, or hybrid? If remote, any technical requirements?

Case theory & defenses
- What is our legal theory and main defenses for the eviction? (e.g., lack of proper notice, payment dispute, warranty of habitability, retaliatory eviction)
- What affirmative defenses should I be ready to present?
- Are there any immigration issues that the judge needs to know about or that could affect outcomes (e.g., relief eligibility, immigration consequences of any eviction finding)?

Evidence and exhibits
- Which documents and exhibits are most important to introduce? (lease, receipts, rent ledger, notices, photos, repair requests, police reports, communications, witness declarations)
- Do we need originals or will copies be fine? How should exhibits be labeled and exchanged with opposing counsel/court?
- Are there any expert reports or declarations we should prepare?

Witnesses and testimony
- Who should testify for us? Do we need the landlord, property manager, witnesses, repair service people, or neighbors?
- Should we prepare written declarations for witnesses who can’t attend?
- What will opposing counsel likely try to impeach about my testimony (dates, payments, credibility)? How should I respond?

Cross-examination & credibility
- What questions is opposing counsel likely to ask me in cross-examination? How should I practice answering them?
- Are there any statements I must avoid or any documents I should not volunteer without discussing first?
- How do we handle prior inconsistent statements or social media posts?

Settlement and alternatives
- Are settlement/mediation options available now? What are realistic settlement outcomes?
- If eviction is unavoidable, can we negotiate timing (stay, move-out schedule) or housing referrals?
- Any immigration- or custody-related consequences to different settlement options?

Immigration and custody intersection (if applicable)
- Could the eviction or anything in this hearing impact my immigration case? If yes, how should we disclose or frame it?
- How might the opposing parent in the custody case try to use eviction/immigration facts against me? What safeguards do we need?
- Should immigration counsel be looped in (if Maria Santos is not immigration counsel)?

Practical hearing prep & logistics
- How long will I have to speak/testify? Is there an order of witnesses?
- Do I need to file a witness list or exhibit list ahead of time?
- How should I dress and behave, and are there courtroom etiquette tips specific to this judge?

Potential emergency motions
- Are there any immediate motions you recommend (e.g., stay, extension, motion to strike evidence, protective order)?
- If the opposing party filed something new after discovery closed, can we move to exclude it?

Costs & strategy
- What are the likely fees, costs, or rent liabilities I might face if we lose?
- What is your recommended strategy and reason for choosing it (litigate vs. negotiate)?

Questions the judge might ask (eviction hearing)
(Short sample answers you can practice — tailor answers with your attorney)
- “Are you the tenant/defendant named in this case?” — Answer: “Yes, I am [full name].”
- “Did you receive the notice to quit/notice to pay or vacate?” — Answer: “Yes/No. I received it on [date]” (have the notice or proof of service ready).
- “Have you paid rent for [month/date]? Can you provide proof?” — Answer: “Yes, here are receipts/bank records” or “No, here is why (with documentation).”
- “Do you have a written lease? What does the lease say about rent/termination?” — Answer: brief factual statement and offer the lease as an exhibit.
- “Have you requested repairs or complained about habitability? What happened?” — Answer: brief timeline and show repair requests, photos, or communications.
- “Are you asking for more time to move out?” — Answer: State the time you need and any reason (work, school, childcare). Provide proposed schedule if possible.

Questions opposing counsel might ask (cross-examination) and how to prepare
- “Isn’t it true you were behind on rent for X months?” — Prepare exact rent ledger and truthful explanation (payments, attempts to pay, stopped income, etc.).
- “Did you authorize that person to live there?” — Be ready with lease/occupancy evidence or explanation.
- “Why didn’t you respond to the notice or court filings?” — Have timeline proof (dates you received documents and actions taken).
- “Isn’t your goal to delay eviction rather than pay/comply?” — Focus on facts, provide remediation plan, and avoid emotional responses.
- “Have you had prior evictions/criminal history?” — Be truthful; discuss how to frame or mitigate with counsel.

Questions the immigration judge might ask (if you have an immigration hearing)
- “What is your immigration status and how long have you been in the U.S.?” — Short factual answer with dates and documents.
- “Are you seeking relief (asylum, cancellation, adjustment) and on what basis?” — Short summary and refer to attorney for legal argument.
- “Have you been convicted of any crimes?” — Be accurate and give dates/outcomes; consult counsel about immigration consequences.
- “Does this eviction or custody matter affect your claims?” — Be careful: answer factually and let counsel frame legal significance.

How to prepare answers (best practices)
- Keep answers short, factual, and consistent. Avoid volunteering extra information.
- Use exact dates when possible; if unsure, say you’ll confirm the date and refer to the exhibit.
- Practice common cross-examination questions aloud with your attorney (role-play).
- Do not argue with the judge — ask to confer with your attorney if you need to clarify.
- If you don’t understand a question, say so and ask for clarification.

Documents and exhibits checklist to bring (copy everything)
- Lease/rental agreement
- Notices (notice to pay, notice to quit, termination)
- Proof of service / complaint and summons
- Payment records: bank statements, money orders, rent ledger
- Communications with landlord (texts, emails, letters)
- Photographs or videos of the unit (dates labeled)
- Repair requests, work orders, invoices
- Police reports or incident reports (if relevant)
- Witness contact info and written statements/declarations
- Any court orders from related proceedings (custody, injunctions)
- ID and immigration documents (if immigration court involved)

Witness prep tips
- Prepare short written declarations summarizing facts and have witnesses review them before hearing.
- Go over direct and cross questions with each witness.
- Make sure witnesses know where and when to appear; arrange for subpoenas if needed.

Red flags to ask your attorney about
- Inadmissible evidence that the judge might allow without a motion
- Contradictory statements you or witnesses made previously (social media, texts)
- Any waiver of rights or inadvertent admissions
- Immigration consequences of admitting facts

Next steps I can help with
- Draft an email to Maria Santos listing these prioritized questions and requesting a meeting or phone call.
- Create a mock Q&A script you can practice aloud for 20–30 minutes with suggested concise answers.
- Make a one-page hearing checklist (documents, exhibits in order) you can print and bring to court.

Would you like me to:
- Draft an email to Maria Santos with the prioritized questions, or
- Create a mock Q&A script tailored to the eviction hearing only, or
- Prepare a printable one-page checklist of documents and exhibits?

Tell me which and I’ll draft it for you.

