Below is a ready-to-use set of things to ask your attorney (Maria Santos), plus likely questions a judge or opposing counsel might ask and practical prep items. Use, adapt, or tell me which case to prioritize and I’ll refine.

A. Questions to ask your attorney (general / must-know)
- What is the exact legal status of my case right now (what’s been filed, what’s due, deadlines)? Confirm dates and response deadlines.
- What is our case theory/strategy going into the hearing or next step?
- What are the realistic outcomes (best case, worst case, most likely)?
- What burden of proof or legal standard applies to this hearing?
- What deadlines should I calendar now (motions, responses, discovery follow-ups, hearing dates)?
- Do you recommend any immediate filings (answer, motion to dismiss, motion for continuance, emergency relief)?
- Can we get an extension or continuance if I need more time to prepare? How likely is the court to grant it?
- Who will speak for me at the hearing (you or me)? If I must testify, how should I behave under oath?
- What communications should I avoid with the opposing party or their counsel?
- What documents and witnesses do we need to rely on — and do we need subpoenas for any witnesses or records?
- What admissions or statements should I avoid making (e.g., promising to pay, admitting fault)?
- Will you prepare me for cross-examination? Can we do a mock session?
- What are the costs and likely timeline if we take it to full hearing vs. settlement/negotiation?
- If there are immigration consequences, how does this hearing interact with my immigration case?
- Are there emergency or temporary remedies I should ask for now (temporary custody, stay of eviction, TRO, stay pending appeal)?
- How will you communicate updates with me (email, phone) and how quickly should I expect responses?

B. Case-specific question bundles — ask Maria these for each relevant case

1) Landlord-tenant eviction
- On what grounds did landlord file eviction (nonpayment, lease violation, holdover, nuisance)?
- Were proper notices given (pay-or-quit, cure, notice to vacate) and were they served correctly under Colorado/DENVER rules?
- Do we have rent payment records, lease, security deposit receipts, repair requests, photographs, written communications (texts/email)?
- Are any local tenant protections or COVID-era moratoria relevant?
- Can we raise defenses like improper notice, landlord’s failure to maintain premises, retaliation, breach of implied warranty of habitability?
- What forms of relief can we request (stay, time to pay, set-aside default, dismissal)?
- If eviction is imminent, what immediate steps protect my housing and belongings?
- If I want to pay rent to stop eviction, how should that be handled to preserve defense?
- If the case is in Denver County, are there local procedures or housing courts I should know about?

2) Immigration court proceedings (if this is a separate matter or impacts the others)
- What is the charge or basis for removal? What relief are we seeking (asylum, cancellation, adjustment)?
- How will the discovery just ended affect hearing evidence (what documents/statements are important)?
- Are there deadlines for filing applications or supporting docs (affidavits, country conditions, medical evidence)?
- What does the government’s file show (A-file, I-213, prior orders) — have we reviewed it?
- What questions does the immigration judge commonly ask in these hearings? (e.g., credibility, entry history, basis for relief)
- How does the ongoing landlord/custody situation affect credibility or relief?
- Do we need expert reports (country conditions, mental health)?
- Are there risks of detention or bond issues, and how do we address them?

3) Custody (parenting / family law) — opposing party is the other parent
- What temporary orders are in place now (custody, support, parenting time)?
- What evidence supports your position on the child’s best interests (school records, medical, witnesses, photos, communication logs)?
- Are there allegations (abuse, neglect, substance use)? If so, how should we respond and what evidence counters them?
- Should we request a parenting evaluation, mediation, or custody investigator? What’s the timeline and cost?
- What will the judge likely ask about parenting, stability, daily routine, and each parent’s relationship with the child?
- Are there protective orders that affect custody or contact?
- How do we coordinate custody issues with immigration or housing instability?

C. Likely questions from the judge or opposing counsel (useful for preparing testimony)
General guidance: answers should be short, truthful, and directly responsive. Don’t volunteer extra info. If you don’t know, say “I don’t know” and offer to have counsel provide it.

Examples — Judge:
- “What relief are you asking the court to grant?”
- “What is your relationship to the other parties involved?”
- “Can you state the relevant dates (when you were served, when the incident occurred, lease start/end, last payment)?”
- “What documents do you have to support your claims/defenses?”
- “Have you attempted to resolve this with the other party?”
- “Why do you need more time or a continuance?”

Examples — Opposing counsel (and cross-examination style questions):
- “Isn’t it true that you did X on [date]?” (be precise; do not agree to anything untrue)
- “Did you ever receive notice about [issue]?” (know when/if notices were received)
- “Why didn’t you do Y sooner?” (explain concisely with facts)
- “Have you ever been convicted of [crime]?” (answer truthfully)
- “Are you currently employed? Where do you live? Who else lives in the home?” (be factual but avoid over-sharing)
- For custody: “Why do you think this parenting plan is in the child’s best interest?” — prepare specific examples/routine details.
- For immigration: “How did you enter the U.S.?” “Why are you afraid to return?” — prepare concise credible responses and corroborating evidence.

D. Documents and evidence checklist to gather now
- Court filings, complaints, summons, notices, and proof of service
- Lease, rent/receipt history, bank records, canceled checks
- Emails, texts, repair requests, photographs, videos with timestamps
- Police reports, protective orders, medical records, school records (custody)
- IDs, immigration A-file copies, prior orders, passports
- Witness contact info and summary statements
- Any admissions or communications from the opposing party that support you

E. Testimony and hearing conduct tips
- Be honest. Never lie under oath — this harms credibility and your case.
- Keep answers short and to the point. Pause before answering.
- Don’t argue with the judge. Let your attorney object if needed.
- If you don’t know an answer, say you don’t know rather than guessing.
- Avoid posting or discussing case details on social media.

F. Next steps I can help with right now
- Draft a focused email to Maria Santos listing the specific questions and attaching the documents you want her to review.
- Create a prioritized checklist of what to gather before the hearing.
- Run a mock Q&A for practicing answers (I can play opposing counsel/judge and give feedback).
- Draft sample responses to typical judge questions (short, plain-language scripts you can rehearse).

If you want, tell me:
- Which case to focus on first (eviction, immigration, or custody).
- Any imminent deadlines (dates).
- Whether you want me to draft an email to Maria Santos now and, if so, what tone (urgent/formal) and any key documents to attach.

I can draft the email or mock-exam questions whenever you’re ready.

