Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
First: quick clarification before I go into specifics — are you asking about one particular case (eviction, immigration, or custody) or do you want prep for all three? They each have different likely questions and documents. Tell me which to prioritize and I’ll tailor the list. Meanwhile, below is a general, organized set of things to ask your attorney and likely questions from a judge or opposing counsel, divided by topic so you can use what’s relevant.

Suggested opening questions to ask Maria Santos
- Which case(s) are we preparing for and what is the hearing type (status conference, merits hearing, removal/eviction hearing, custody hearing)?
- What’s the exact date, time, and courtroom — and who is the judge?
- What is our legal position / theory and the key facts we must prove?
- What is the specific standard of proof for this hearing and who has the burden?
- What documents and witnesses do we need to bring and how many copies should I bring for the court/opposing counsel/you?
- Are there any outstanding deadlines or filings I must complete before the hearing?
- What questions should I expect from the judge vs. opposing counsel?
- What admissions/answers are risky? What should I avoid saying?
- If opposing counsel asks for settlement or mediation, what authority do I have to respond?
- What will happen if I miss the hearing or miss a filing deadline?
- Do I need an interpreter, and if so, will the court provide one or do we arrange one?
- Can we run a mock Q&A (role play) before the hearing and review my written statement?
- Are there forms I should file or declarations/affidavits I should sign beforehand?
- Are there safety or confidentiality concerns I should raise (e.g., domestic violence, immigration consequences)?

Documents and evidence checklist to confirm with your attorney
- Chronological timeline of events (dates, short notes).
- Copies of all pleadings, notices, summons, and proof of service.
- Leases, rent ledgers, repair requests, photos, texts/emails, receipts, bank records.
- Police reports, protection orders, medical records, child welfare records (if custody/domestic violence involved).
- Witness list with short summaries of what each witness will say and their contact info.
- Declarations or affidavits signed under penalty of perjury (if applicable).
- Discovery responses and any outstanding discovery disputes.
- Exhibits labeled and organized for easy reference (and copies for everyone).
- Evidence establishing legal status or immigration relief (for immigration court).
- Any expert reports (e.g., home inspectors, psychologists).

Likely questions from the judge (general)
- What relief are you requesting?
- What facts support your request?
- What evidence are you offering?
- Have you attempted to resolve this with the other side?
- Are there any outstanding discovery or procedural issues?
- Do you understand the consequences if the court rules against you?
- Do you have any scheduling or access issues to present?
- Are you requesting continuance or more time? Why?

Likely lines of questioning from opposing counsel (general)
- Questions testing your timeline and consistency: “When exactly did X happen?” “Did you say Y at that time?”
- Questions probing motive or credibility: “Why didn’t you do Z?” or “Why did you wait until now?”
- Questions designed to narrow or limit your claims: “Do you have evidence for that specific claim?”
- Questions aiming to create admissions: short yes/no questions about facts you may not want to admit.
- If custody: questions about parenting time, care routines, drug/alcohol use, criminal history, child’s schooling/medical needs.
- If eviction: questions about rent payment history, lease terms, notices received, habitability complaints and responses.
- If immigration: questions about identity, dates, country conditions, prior statements to immigration or other agencies.

Case-specific likely questions and prep (brief)

- Eviction / landlord-tenant
  - Judge/opposing counsel may ask: Did you receive a written notice? When did you pay rent last? Did you contact the landlord about repairs? Have you lived there continuously since X date?
  - Prepare: rent receipts/ledgers, notices, photos of habitability issues, communications with landlord, witnesses (neighbors), repair invoices.

- Immigration (removal/proceedings)
  - Judge/opposing counsel may ask: What is your immigration status? On what basis are you seeking relief? What are the dates/locations relevant to your claim? Did you disclose this information previously to any agency?
  - Prepare: identity documents, application forms, previous statements, country condition evidence, witness affidavits, timeline of immigration events.
  - Ask attorney: What forms of relief are we pursuing? What evidence meets the legal standard? What are the deadlines for applications or briefs?

- Custody / family law
  - Judge/opposing counsel may ask: What is the child’s current living arrangement? Who provides care and support? Have there been any allegations of abuse, neglect, substance use? What is your proposed parenting schedule?
  - Prepare: school records, medical records, witness statements, communications about parenting, any protective orders.
  - Ask attorney: Will there be custody evaluations or home visits? How do we present the child’s best interests?

How to handle tough questions in the hearing
- Pause before answering; ask to clarify the question if unclear.
- Keep answers short, factual, and to the point. Avoid volunteering extra information.
- If you don’t remember, say you don’t recall rather than guessing.
- If a question invites speculation, say you don’t have first-hand knowledge.
- Defer legal arguments to your attorney; focus on factual answers.
- If you’re concerned a question could incriminate you (immigration/criminal overlap), ask your attorney whether to exercise any rights (e.g., counsel about answering).

Practical hearing-day checklist
- Bring multiple copies of exhibits and a list of exhibits.
- Bring ID, case number, and any court notices.
- Arrive early and dress conservatively.
- Bring notes or a short prepared statement (consult attorney on whether to submit one).
- Confirm cell phone off policy and childcare if needed.
- Get the judge’s name and courtroom number ahead of time.
- Coordinate with your attorney about who speaks when.

Questions to ask your attorney to prepare you for cross-examination
- What questions will opposing counsel likely ask me to undermine my credibility?
- Which of my past statements could be used against me and how should I address them?
- What inconsistencies should I expect and how should I respond?
- Are there any “red flag” facts I should avoid discussing without your advice?
- How should I handle leading or compound questions?
- Are there specific phrases I should avoid so as not to create harmful admissions?

Next steps I can help with
- I can turn this into a concise list/email you can send to Maria Santos asking for a prep session and answers to the specific items above — would you like me to draft that email? If so, tell me whether to send or save as a draft and whether to include any personal details or documents.
- I can make a short one-page hearing prep checklist you can print and bring to court.
- If you tell me which case to prioritize (eviction, immigration, or custody), I’ll create a tailored list of likely questions the judge and opposing counsel might ask and suggested short, safe answers to practice.

Final note: I’m not your lawyer. This is general preparation help — confirm all strategy and legal answers with Maria Santos before the hearing. Do you want me to draft the email to Maria with these questions now? If yes, say “Draft email” and any specifics to include.

