Quick summary I’m working from (tell me if anything is off): landlord-tenant eviction matter; proceedings are in immigration court as well; opposing party is the other parent in a custody case; case is in Denver County, Colorado; you’ve been served and have a short deadline to respond; discovery just ended and you’re waiting for a hearing date.

Suggested questions to ask your attorney (grouped and with suggested wording)

1) Case status, deadlines, and next steps
- “Can you explain the current procedural posture of my case—what’s finished, what’s next, and what firm deadlines do I have?”
- “What is the deadline for my response, and can we file any motions or extensions if needed?”
- “When will we likely receive a hearing date and how will you notify me?”

2) Strategy and goals
- “What is our overall strategy for the eviction/immigration/custody overlap?”
- “What outcome do you recommend we aim for at the hearing (dismissal, settlement, continuance, mitigation) and why?”
- “Are there settlement or mediation options we should pursue before the hearing?”

3) Evidence and documents you must provide
- “What documents do you need from me now? Please make a checklist I can follow.”
  (Examples to confirm: lease or rental agreement, any notices (pay or quit, eviction notice), proof of rent payments, bank records, text/email logs, photos of property condition, repair requests, police reports, court orders from custody or immigration, identity/immigration documents, discovery responses.)
- “Do we have everything from discovery? If not, what’s missing and how do we obtain it?”

4) How the judge and opposing counsel might question you (prep)
- “What specific questions should I expect the judge to ask me at the hearing?”
- “What questions is opposing counsel likely to ask during cross-examination?”
- “How should I answer questions to be clear, concise, and avoid accidentally giving harmful information?”

5) Witnesses and testimony
- “Should I testify? What should I say and what should I avoid saying?”
- “Do we need witnesses or declarations/affidavits? If so, who and what should their statements include?”
- “How should I prepare any witnesses for cross-examination?”

6) Immigration consequences and coordination
- “How could this eviction or the pending proceedings affect my immigration case or status?”
- “Do we need to inform the immigration attorney (if different) or coordinate strategy with them?”
- “Are there immigration-specific documents or evidence we should submit?”

7) Custody interplay (if relevant)
- “How does the opposing parent’s involvement or custody issues affect this case?”
- “Could arguments or evidence in eviction affect the custody proceeding (or vice versa)?”
- “Should we notify the family/custody attorney and coordinate?”

8) Court logistics and behavior
- “Where exactly is the hearing and how early should I arrive?”
- “What should I bring to court and how should I dress?”
- “Will you appear with me or will I need to appear alone? If you appear, will you do oral argument?”

9) Possible rulings and consequences
- “What are the possible outcomes and their immediate consequences (eviction order, monetary judgment, bond, immigration referrals)?”
- “If we lose, what are my options for appeal or motion for reconsideration and the timelines for those?”

10) Communication and costs
- “What’s the best way to reach you if something urgent comes up, and what response time should I expect?”
- “What are the expected costs between now and the hearing (filing fees, expert fees, copying, etc.)?”
- “If we need additional discovery or an expert, how quickly can we get that arranged?”

11) Practical immediate tasks (short window actions)
- “Given the short response window, what should I do right now to protect my rights?”
- “Can you file a temporary stay or emergency motion if service or timing was improper?”
- “Should I avoid contacting the opposing party directly?”

What the judge (or opposing counsel) may ask — sample questions you should be ready to answer briefly and truthfully
- “Can you state your full name for the record and your relationship to this property/child?”
- “Were you served? When and how were you served?”
- “What is your current address and how long have you lived there?”
- “Have you received any notices from the landlord? What did they say and when?”
- “Have you paid rent? Can you provide proof of payments?”
- “What is your version of the facts that led to this eviction or dispute?”
- “Are there any repairs or habitability issues you reported? What did the landlord do?”
- “Has the opposing party ever threatened you or attempted to evict you outside the legal process?”
- For immigration-related hearings: “What is your immigration status; do you have pending applications or proceedings?”
- For custody overlap: “Does this dispute affect your parenting time or relationship with the child? How?”

How to prepare answers (tips)
- Keep answers short, factual, and to the point.
- Don’t volunteer extra information or speculate — if you don’t know, say so.
- Avoid blaming language, accusations, or hearsay unless you have evidence.
- Rehearse concise versions of your story (2–3 sentences) and key facts with dates.
- Bring organized copies of any documents and highlight the key pages.

Documents checklist to bring to meeting/hearing
- Summons and complaint / eviction notice / service documents
- Lease / rental agreement and any amendments
- Receipts and bank statements showing rent payments
- All written communications with landlord/opposing party (texts, emails)
- Photographs or videos with dates showing property condition
- Police or incident reports, repair requests, maintenance logs
- Court orders from custody or immigration (if relevant)
- Discovery responses and documents produced by either side
- ID and immigration documents (passports, receipts, filings)
- Witness contact info and written witness statements (if available)

Questions to ask Maria about trial prep and testimony phrasing
- “Can we role-play the hearing questions so I can practice my answers?”
- “Can you draft key talking points for me to use in court?”
- “Which facts should I emphasize and which ones should I avoid discussing?”
- “Will you object to improper questions during cross-exam? If so, which objections might you use?”

Checklist: immediate next actions you can ask your attorney to do
- Confirm filing deadlines and, if necessary, file responsive pleadings or motions to extend.
- Request hearing date and whether we can obtain a continuance if needed.
- Confirm what documents you must provide and set a deadline to send them.
- Decide whether to attempt settlement/mediation and how to approach that.
- Schedule a prep meeting / mock examination before the hearing.

Would you like me to:
