Below is a ready-to-use list of questions and topics to ask Maria Santos (or to bring up at your next contact) about what the judge or opposing counsel might ask and how to prepare. You can use these as a checklist in a meeting, on a call, or in an email.

Questions to ask your attorney about likely judge / opposing counsel questions and preparation
- Case status & timeline
  - What is the exact status of each case (eviction, custody, immigration)? What hearings or deadlines are next?
  - What are the deadlines for filing any responses, motions, or evidence?
  - How long do you expect the hearing to be, and what is the likely date range?

- Scope and key issues the judge will focus on
  - What specific legal issues will the judge be deciding at the next hearing?
  - What facts about my situation will the judge likely ask me to explain or prove?
  - Are there legal technicalities (service, jurisdiction, timeliness, standing) the judge may raise?

- What opposing counsel is likely to ask
  - What are the strengths of their case and what specific facts or admissions will they push me to concede?
  - What types of cross-examination or leading questions should I expect from opposing counsel?
  - Which statements or admissions should I avoid making on the record?

- Evidence & documents
  - Which documents should I bring to the hearing (lease, payment receipts, photos, repair requests, text/email communications, custody-related records, immigration documents)?
  - Which documents do you plan to file or present as exhibits? Are any authentication steps required?
  - Should we prepare a timeline or exhibit binder for the judge?

- Witnesses and testimony
  - Who should we call as witnesses (neighbors, co-tenants, witnesses to incidents, childcare providers, character witnesses)? Who could be harmful to call?
  - How should I prepare witnesses? Do you want to do a prep/moot session or mock cross-examination?
  - Will you testify or represent me in court?

- Discovery & outstanding items
  - Are there any outstanding discovery obligations or responses I need to provide now?
  - Are there motions we should consider (motion to compel, motion to dismiss, motion in limine, protective order)?

- Strategy & risks
  - What is our overall strategy for the hearing? What are the best and worst outcomes?
  - What are potential immigration consequences (if relevant) — e.g., does an eviction or custody ruling affect my immigration case? Should immigration counsel be consulted or looped in?
  - What settlement options exist and what would be the terms to accept vs. reject?

- Courtroom conduct and preparation for you
  - How should I answer questions from the judge or opposing counsel? (brief, direct, don’t volunteer extra facts)
  - Are there phrases or admissions to avoid?
  - What should I wear, and what ID/documents should I bring to court?
  - Do you want me to prepare a short statement or opening summary?

- Pleadings & filings
  - Have we filed the required responses? If not, what should I do immediately about the short window to respond?
  - If we need an extension or continuance, how likely is the judge to grant it and how do we request it?

- Practical logistics
  - Where and when will the hearing occur (courtroom number, virtual link if remote)?
  - How early should I arrive? Can I bring someone for support?
  - What length of time is typical for this judge’s hearings?

- Communication & follow-up
  - How will you communicate updates to me? What is the best way to reach you if something urgent happens?
  - What do you need from me right now (documents, signed forms, contact info for witnesses, timeline of events)?

